== 4. In Secret We May Freely Unbosom Our Souls to God

Fourthly, consider that in secret we may unbosom our souls to God more freely, and fully, and safely than we can in the presence of many or a few. That is why the husband is to mourn apart, and the wife apart (Zech 12:12–14), not only to show the soundness of their sorrow, but also to show their sincerity by their secrecy. They must mourn apart so that their sins may not be disclosed or revealed to one another. Here they are separated to show that they did not weep for company’s sake, but for their own particular sins, by which they had pierced and crucified the Lord of glory. In secret a Christian may go into such particulars as in public or before others he will not, he may not, he ought not to mention. Ah, how many Christians there are who would blush and be ashamed to walk in the streets, and to keep company with sinners or saints, if the infirmities, enormities, and wickednesses which they freely and fully lay open to God in secret were so much as written on their foreheads, or known to others! There are many sins which many men have fallen into before conversion and since conversion which, if they were known to the world, would make themselves stink, and religion stink, and their profession of faith stink in the nostrils of all who know them. Yes, if the weaknesses and wickednesses which many are guilty of before grace received, or since grace received, were proclaimed on the housetops, how weak Christians would be staggered, young beginners in the ways of God discouraged, many mouths of blasphemy opened, and many sinners’ hearts hardened against the Lord, His ways, His reproofs, and the things of their own peace! Yes, how Satan’s banner would be displayed, and his kingdom strengthened, and he himself infinitely pleased and delighted! It is an infinite mercy in God, and His gracious stooping to us, to lay a law of restraint on Satan, who otherwise would be the greatest telltale in all the world. It would be mirth and music to him to be always laying open the follies and weaknesses of the saints.

Ambrose brings in the devil boasting against Christ, and claiming Judas as his own: “He is not Yours, Lord Jesus, he is mine. His thoughts beat for me; he eats with You, but is fed by me; he takes bread from You, but money from me; he drinks with You, and sells Your blood to me.” There is not a sin that a saint commits but Satan would trumpet it out to all the world if God would only give him leave. No man in his right mind will lay open to everyone his bodily infirmities, weaknesses, diseases, ailments, griefs, etc., but only to some near relative, or bosom friend, or skilled physician. So no man in his right mind will lay open to everyone the infirmities, weaknesses, diseases, ailments, griefs, etc., of his soul, but only to the Lord, or to some particular person who is wise, faithful, and able to contribute something to his soul’s relief. If a Christian were to lay open or rip open all his follies and vanities to the world, how cruelly some would mock and scorn him! And how severely and bitterly others would censure and judge him! When David was alone in the cave, then he poured out his complaint to God, and showed his trouble before Him (Ps 142:2). And when Job was all alone, then his eyes poured out tears to God (Job 16:20). There is no hazard, no danger, in ripping everything open before God in a corner, but there may be a great deal of hazard and danger in ripping everything open before men.
